## PART 2: DOJ CFAA POLICY - GOOD-FAITH SECURITY RESEARCH IS EXEMPT FROM PROSECUTION

### DOJ Announcement (May 19, 2022)

The Department of Justice revised its charging policy for the Computer Fraud and Abuse Act (CFAA) to direct that **good-faith security research should not be charged** [citation:2][citation:5][citation:8].

Deputy Attorney General Lisa Monaco stated [citation:10]:

> *"Computer security research is a key driver of improved cybersecurity. The department has never been interested in prosecuting good-faith computer security research as a crime."*

### Legal Definition of "Good-Faith Security Research"

Under the DOJ policy, security research is considered good faith when ALL of the following circumstances are met [citation:2][citation:5][citation:8]:

| Element | Requirement |
|---------|-------------|
| **Purpose** | Accessing a computer solely for good-faith testing, investigation, or correction of a security flaw or vulnerability |
| **Harm Avoidance** | Activity is carried out in a manner designed to avoid any harm to individuals or the public |
| **Public Benefit** | Information derived is used primarily to promote security or safety of the class of devices, machines, or online services accessed |

### What the Policy Explicitly Protects

The DOJ policy provides protection from prosecution for [citation:5]:
- Embellishing an online dating profile contrary to terms of service
- Creating fictional accounts on hiring, housing, or rental websites
- Using a pseudonym on a social networking site that prohibits them
- Checking sports scores at work
- Paying bills at work
- Violating an access restriction contained in a term of service

### What is NOT Protected (Bad Faith)

The DOJ explicitly warns [citation:2][citation:8][citation:10]:
> *"Claiming to be conducting security research is not a free pass for those acting in bad faith... discovering vulnerabilities in devices in order to extort their owners, even if claimed as 'research,' is not in good faith."*

### Procedural Protection

Federal prosecutors must consult with the Computer Crime and Intellectual Property Section (CCIPS) before bringing any CFAA charges [citation:5][citation:10]. If CCIPS recommends declining charges, prosecutors must inform the Office of the Deputy Attorney General.

