## PART 3: VAN BUREN V. UNITED STATES (2021) - SUPREME COURT NARROWS CFAA

### The Holding

The Supreme Court held that an individual **"exceeds authorized access"** under the CFAA **only** when [citation:3][citation:6][citation:7]:

> *"He accesses a computer with authorization but then obtains information located in particular areas of the computer—such as files, folders, or databases—that are off-limits to him."*

### What Van Buren Means for Security Researchers

| Activity | Legal Status Under Van Buren |
|----------|------------------------------|
| Using valid credentials to access data you CAN access | ✅ **NOT a CFAA violation** even if purpose is improper |
| Violating employer policy or terms of service | ✅ **NOT a CFAA violation** |
| Accessing data from a folder/database you have permission to enter | ✅ **NOT a CFAA violation** regardless of motive |
| Accessing a folder/database you are literally prohibited from entering | ❌ **MAY BE a CFAA violation** |

### The "Gates-Up-or-Down" Inquiry

The Supreme Court established a simple test [citation:7][citation:9]:

> *"Liability under both clauses stems from a gates-up-or-down inquiry—one either can or cannot access a computer system, and one either can or cannot access certain areas within the system."*

**This completely rejects** the government's argument that improper purpose or policy violations create criminal liability.

### The Slippery Slope the Court Rejected

The Court noted that the government's interpretation [citation:6][citation:7]:

> *"Would attach criminal penalties to a breathtaking amount of commonplace computer activity. For instance, employers commonly state that computers and electronic devices can be used only for business purposes. On the Government's reading, an employee who sends a personal e-mail or reads the news using a work computer has violated the CFAA."*

